For near-normal viewing,


d_{apparent}=frac{d_{real}}{n}


Since the refractive index is higher for blue light, its apparent depth is smaller. The difference arises from dispersion, not from a change in frequency at the water–air boundary.

n=frac{d_{real}}{d_{apparent}}



d_{real}=1.5×4=6 cm


The actual depth is greater than the apparent depth. Option A reverses the depth ratio.

sin C=frac1{sqrt2}


